class Solution {
  public int recursiveShelvesPositiveInkInvoice(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {class Walk {void visit(int l,int r){if(l>=r)return;int s=0;for(int i=l;i<r;i++)s+=a[i];out.add(s);if(r-l>1){int m=(l+r)/2;visit(l,m);visit(m,r);}}}new Walk().visit(0,a.length);}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)if(x>0)answer+=x;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

Recursively inspect half-open ranges [left,right), starting with the whole array. For each nonempty range, record its sum, then inspect its left half and right half using mid=floor((left+right)/2); stop splitting a one-element range. Produce the recorded sums in that preorder.

Return the sum of strictly positive values in the derived sequence, or 0 when none exist.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(n log n); space O(n).

